import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Home">
  <img class="banner light-only" src="/images/banners/banner light.png" alt="Rangitoto Informatics" width="1536" height="512" />
  <img class="banner dark-only" src="/images/banners/banner dark.png" alt="Rangitoto Informatics" width="1536" height="512" />
  
  <h2>What is informatics?</h2>
  <p>
  Informatics is basically programming, but competitive.
  You are given a problem and tasked to write code that takes input and prints an output that matches the problem's description.
  Your score is determined by how your output matches the expected output and how much resources your code takes up.
  </p>

  <h2>What do we do?</h2>
  <p>
  We are an enthusiastic group of people sharing an interest in informatics!
  At Informatics Club, we prepare you for two competitions:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>The New Zealand Informatics Competition (NZIC)</li>
    <li>The New Zealand Programming Competition (NZPC)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
  Near the end of the year, we will also cover tips and tricks to help you in the NZQA Digital Technologies Scholarship exam.
  <br>
  If you just want to learn for fun, you are more than welcome to stay.
  <br>
  Python and C++ are the two main languages we use.
  </p>

  <h2>Where can you find us?</h2>
  <p>
  Weekly on Monday lunchtimes in A25!
  </p>

  <br>
  <hr>

  <h3>More about the NZIC</h3>
  <p>
  The NZIC is an online programming contest series, run by the New Zealand Olympiad in Informatics.
  There will be 3 rounds, each lasting 3 hours.
  In each round, you may choose a time during the week of that round to solve a selection of programming problems.
  More information can be found on their <a href="https://www.nzoi.org.nz/nzic/">website</a>.
  The contest will be held on their <a href="https://train.nzoi.org.nz/">training website</a>, where you can also find past problems.
  </p>

  <h3>More about the NZPC</h3>
  <p>
  The NZPC is an annual team programming contest, held at Auckland University.
  Teams of up to three contestants have 5 hours to solve as many problems as they can on a single computer.
  More information can be found on their <a href="https://nzprogcontest.org.nz/">website</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>


